HERE WRITE WHAT IS NEEDED WHEN GOING TO A FTC COMPETION(judging session):

we need to create physical documentation(also known as the engineering portfolio)
- the page limit is 15 pages(otherwise the judges will ignore it)
- it should contain our robots story(show how we fixed things when they broke and how we built the robot using pictures and visual stuff)
- show how we wrote the code
- show how we used math and physics for our mechinisms
- show our commuinity outreach(how we helped others)

We need to make a 5 minute presentation showing:
- our idenity
- how robots journey, design circle, what we have learned, and how we arrived to our final robot
- we can bring visual props and media, prototype mechinisms, and posters(well duh)

We should bring the full engineering noteback(as back-up)
- we have to keep it visible sense the judges may want to verify the authenticity of our engineering habits or to see the deeper math/calculations.
- they will ask to look at our full notebook during pit inteviews

PIT INTERVIEWS ARE: follow up conversations the happen right at our team's workstation (or our "pit") during the competition.
During the pit interviews we have to show gracious professionalism, and always be read to explain what problem we are working on/solving.
Pit interviews are important because they are where judged awards are won or lost. 

The pit interviews take place usually throughout the main matches and competition after the  morning judging sessions are finished

The pit interviews are also important because if judges visit your pit to ask specific questions, it means your team made the short list for an award based on your morning presentation or portfolio. 
They are there to take a closer look and decide if you deserve a 1st, 2nd, or 3rd place trophy.


